hello John Perry here you are watching  the stated casually Channel welcome to  my new miniseries evidence for evolution  in your own backyard you know we often  think that we need to go to a museum or  dig up fossils to find evidence but it's  actually all around us all of the time  in this series I will show you things  that have caught my eye either literally  in my yard or at least in the immediate  world around me in the woods or at the  park or in the pond nearby my house even  in the gardening shop which is what  we'll look at today actually yesterday I  dropped a friend off at the airport and  on the way home I stopped by a little  roadside gardening shop just to kind of  stretch my legs and you know see what  they had around in there and I found two  plants two succulent species that I had  never seen before  one was a plant native to Madagascar  named mother of thousands it's called  that because it appears to have dozens  of quote babies growing off of its  leaves and you actually can plant them  they'll turn into full adult plants but  that's another story for another time  the plant of interest here was a second  plant one native to the extremely dry  environments of South West Africa and  it's popularly called the string of  pearls  it has spear shaped leaves that at first  I mistook for fruits or some sort of  seed pod now knowing next to nothing  about plants and their evolution I  immediately got out my phone and started  reading first Wikipedia and then I  wandered on to a bunch of scientific  journals I started reading the actual  primary research about these plants and  it turns out that a succulent and by the  way this was news to me is any plant  that has evolved elastic swellable water  holding flesh either in its stems  branches leaves or all three flesh that  allows the plant to store water to  survive in dry climates the thing about  this that was new to me the thing I  didn't know before reading about this is  that succulents do not belong to a  single evolutionary clade biologists  used to think that they did and I used  to think that they did but by carefully  comparing different traits reproductive  strategies flower Anatomy different ways  in which water is actually stored in the  tissues of these plants  and then also the genetics now that we  can compare genomes all these studies  have shown scientists that the succulent  trait has actually evolved multiple  times independently in different tissues  of different plants now this actually  sort of makes sense because all plants  and animals of course hold water in  their tissues which can shrink up a  little bit or swell up a little bit  depending on how much water there is  this means that all plants are already  sort of primed to evolve this trait when  needed the extreme water holding tissues  and succulents are just a variation that  often evolves when plants have to deal  with extremely dry environments after  learning this I was curious are there  intermediate species in my own backyard  are there plants that are sort of like  succulents but not really quite  succulents and sure enough I found one  this plant right here I have no clue  what species it is it is extremely  rubbery here my curiosity got the best  of me and I started pulling apart part  of the flower stem here and it's it's  amazing how much liquid there is inside  especially compared to the other plants  in the yard and when I broke up in one  of the leaves it was filled with this  gel-like substance so here we have a  plant that's not I wouldn't really call  it a succulent but it's very succulent  like beautiful example of a transitional  species by the way if you happen to know  what this species is let me know in the  comments section I'd love to learn more  about it most plants that we call cacti  or cactuses are also considered to be  succulents stem succulents because they  hold water in their stems  cactus leaves and most species have been  modified into spines and are almost  completely dry most house plants that  are marketed as succulents are actually  what we call  leaf succulents the flesh of the leaves  is the part that is swollen up to  contain all the water of all the leaf  succulents in existence the string of  pearls has to be the most extreme  whereas most plants have flat leaves it  has the exact opposite of a flat leaf it  has a perfectly spherical leaf nearly  perfectly spherical each leaf has a  sliver of clear tissue running along the  top called a leaf window the function of  a leaf in most plants is of course to  capture as much sunlight as  at a safe intensity for photosynthesis  being thin and flat gives most leaves a  huge surface area to collect that  sunlight the drawback of course is that  flat leaves also have a lot of surface  area where they can lose water to  evaporation a sphere of course is the  best possible shape if you want to avoid  evaporation because it has the smallest  possible surface area to volume ratio of  any other shape that's great when it  comes to water conservation in the  desert but it also means that there is  less surface area to collect sunlight  that's where most web sites claim that  the leaf window comes in the section of  clear skin you see here which sort of  glows when the plant is backlit like  this super pretty this is a fairly rare  trait in plants it's usually only found  in succulent species that hide most of  their body underground here the windows  function is fairly obvious it focuses  light down into the leaf so the plant  can perform photosynthesis even though  it's hiding from the outside world  people assumed that string of pearl  succulents were doing the same thing the  windows were an adaptation to absorb  more light through the leaf but a series  of experiments trying to test this claim  found that if you cover the leaf windows  it does not affect the plant's ability  to photosynthesize it might be that in  this species leaf windows are simply  belly buttons they're just a byproduct  of leaf development and they're useless  in mature leaves I actually have an  alternative idea which I'll test during  a leaf dissection in a future video  if the photosynthetic tissue is more  rigid than the window tissue the window  might simply serve to relieve tension so  the leaves can swell and contract safely  when they absorb and lose water if you  want to see that dissection make sure  you're subscribed to my youtube channel  and click the little notification bell  now whether or not the leaf window  serves a survival function or it's just  a byproduct of development it's obvious  that the leaves of this strange plant  are highly specialized for maximum water  storage with this in mind I thought to  myself if evolution is true and if this  plant has close living relatives those  relatives should represent intermediates  something between the string of pearls  perfectly round leaves  and the more typical flat leaves of  other plants so I did a quick little  google search to figure out which plants  are most closely related and boom  hypothesis confirmed its closest known  cousin is senecio radicans its leaves  are far less specialized in shape making  them a near-perfect intermediate between  a more typical plant and the extreme  string of pearls succulent it even has a  leaf window that seems to be  intermediate as if that weren't enough  evidence that these succulents must have  evolved from more typical plants that  existed in past here is another close  relative also living in southern Africa  senecio Barbour tonica's its leaves are  less specialized still and in place of a  leaf window and has a simple groove on  top of each leaf from this we can use a  bit of inductive reasoning to conclude  not with certainty but at least with a  fair amount of confidence that the  string of Pearl succulent with its  highly unique ball like leaves and well  the fine leaf windows evolved from a  less extreme ancestor similar to senecio  Barbour tonica's as it slowly adapted to  drier and drier climates so there you  have it a bit of evidence for evolution  in your own backyard or at least in a  gardening center near you I'm John Perry  stay curious  it makes long drives to and from the  airport way more entertaining  let me show off my brand-new t-shirt  designed by artist Jordan Culver you can  purchase one at the link below in the  video description and by doing so you  support my channel's the stated casually  channel which are watching right now and  these stated clearly channel the t-shirt  features a Corvette acro hanging out  with his long-lost cousin a  dromaeosaurus  and it comes in the you know typical  t-shirt and then uh women's cut as well  so yeah go check it out link is in the  video description  you
 

